
## 1. FAISS — Complete Overview

### What It Is
- **Library name:** FAISS (Facebook AI Similarity Search)
- **Developer:** Meta’s FAIR team (Facebook AI Research)
- **Purpose:** Efficient similarity search and clustering of dense vectors
- **Language:** Primarily C++ with full Python bindings (NumPy compatible)
- **License:** MIT (free for commercial use)

### Core Capabilities
- **Exact search** (guaranteed correct, slower for large data)
- **Approximate search** (very fast, tiny accuracy loss)
- **GPU acceleration** (multiple GPUs supported)
- **Distributed search** (across multiple machines)
- **Clustering** (k-means, k-means++)

### What FAISS Is NOT
- ❌ Not a vector database (no persistence, concurrent writes, or built-in data management)
- ❌ Not a key-value store (you must manage mapping from indices → original data yourself)
- ❌ No built-in embedding generation (you need separate models)

### When to Use FAISS
| Use Case | Good Fit? | Notes |
|----------|-----------|-------|
| Offline batch search | ✅ Excellent | Research, prototyping, static datasets |
| Real-time API (<50ms) | ✅ Good (with IVF/HNSW) | If dataset <50M vectors |
| Production with updates | ⚠️ Moderate | You must rebuild index or use incremental add |
| Multi-tenant | ❌ Poor | No built-in partitioning |
| Persistent storage needed | ❌ Poor | Use FAISS + PostgreSQL pgvector / Pinecone / Weaviate |

